WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.720
A kidney cell has no business at a synapse.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:13.040
Yet make it produce one neuronal protein, called neuroligin, and axons that touch it

3
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.580
in a dish act as if they had found a partner.

4
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.800
What do those axons do?

5
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.100
And does the brain need neuroligin to do the same?

6
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.100
Each contact in the brain has to say what kind of synapse it will become.

7
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:29.873
Grow kidney cells under axons.

8
00:00:30.453 --> 00:00:36.113
Where an axon crosses a cell that makes neuroligin, synaptic vesicles gather at the

9
00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:39.433
contact. Where it crosses other cells, they do not.

10
00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:44.033
The axon also releases those vesicles and takes them back.

11
00:00:44.853 --> 00:00:50.493
A dye taken up during release marks the working terminals, so the cluster is more

12
00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:51.613
than a pile of parts.

13
00:00:52.867 --> 00:00:55.807
Neurexins sit on the axon side.

14
00:00:56.807 --> 00:00:59.287
Neuroligins are their partners on the other side.

15
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:02.827
Now add soluble neurexin to the dish.

16
00:01:03.607 --> 00:01:08.587
It binds neuroligin first, so the axon's own neurexin cannot reach it.

17
00:01:09.347 --> 00:01:11.087
The vesicle clusters are blocked.

18
00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:16.087
So the effect runs through the bond between neurexin and neuroligin.

19
00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:21.477
So neuroligin can do this, on a bare cell, in a dish.

20
00:01:22.217 --> 00:01:23.217
Call that sufficient.

21
00:01:23.977 --> 00:01:26.177
It is a clean test of one protein.

22
00:01:26.957 --> 00:01:29.797
But a dish tests what one protein can do.

23
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:32.697
It does not test what the brain depends on.

24
00:01:33.497 --> 00:01:39.077
For that, you need a genetic experiment: take the protein away from the mouse and

25
00:01:39.077 --> 00:01:40.077
look at the synapses.

26
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:47.910
Mice lacking neuroligins 1, 2 and 3 were reported to have synapses in normal number

27
00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.650
and structure in the brainstem.

28
00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:54.550
But transmission was impaired, and the mice died from failed breathing.

29
00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:00.150
Mice lacking all three alpha-neurexins died soon after birth.

30
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:05.950
Their synapses were present at close to normal density, yet released transmitter

31
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.450
poorly. Synapses formed.

32
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.150
They worked badly.

33
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:16.930
So alpha-neurexins and neuroligins are needed for normal synapse function and

34
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.010
maturation, not for forming most synapses.

35
00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.830
Many organizers overlap.

36
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:28.450
A dish with one organizer on a bare cell tests what that one can do.

37
00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:34.470
Keep the rule. Sufficient in a dish is not the same as required in the animal.
